# tools/source_roots.local.example -- COPY THIS TO tools/source_roots.local AND EDIT.
#
# THIS FILE (.example) IS TRACKED. THE FILE YOU MAKE FROM IT (.local) IS NOT, and .gitignore
# names it as a literal. That split is the whole point of the mechanism:
#
#     THE IDENTITY OF A SOURCE TRAVELS IN THE REPOSITORY; THE LOCATION OF IT DOES NOT.
#
# This repository ships the summaries it wrote. It never ships the publications it summarises --
# no PDF enters version control, `_intake/` and `literature/_pdf/` are gitignored, and the trees
# named below are read-only to every tool here. So a summary cannot carry the path of its source:
# a path resolves only on the machine it was typed on. It carries an IDENTITY instead, and this
# file is where a given machine says where that identity lives.
#
# YOU DO NOT NEED THIS FILE. A clone without it is correct and complete. It will report every
# external source as `unresolvable here`, which is a fact about your machine and not a defect in
# the corpus, and `node tools/verify_corpus.js` will not fail because of it. Create this file only
# if you hold the source trees and want the checks to actually open them.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- WHAT A LINE MEANS
#
#   # as: <alias>          A DIRECTIVE, not decoration. It binds the alias to the NEXT path line.
#                          The alias is the string that appears in a summary's
#                          `- **Source:** ` + "`" + `<alias>:<path under the root>` + "`" + ` cell.
#                          Aliases are lower-case, at least two characters, [a-z0-9-].
#
#   <absolute path>        One root, one line, absolute, no quotes, no trailing comment. A line
#                          with no `# as:` directive above it is an UNALIASED root: it is used by
#                          tools/audit_abstract_overlap.js, which searches all roots by filename,
#                          and is ignored by the alias resolver in tools/verify_corpus.js.
#
#   (blank line)           Cancels a pending `# as:` directive, so a dangling one at the foot of a
#                          section cannot capture an unrelated root further down.
#
# WHY THE DIRECTIVE IS A COMMENT. tools/audit_abstract_overlap.js reads this same file as a list of
# bare paths and skips `#` lines. Writing the alias as `japanese-miracle = C:\...` instead would
# make that reader treat the whole line as a path, fail to find it, drop the root, and silently
# shrink the population it measures. The comment form is byte-compatible with both readers.
#
# AN ALIAS MAY BE DECLARED MORE THAN ONCE. The roots are tried in the order written, first hit
# wins. Order is load-bearing when two copies of a source tree have drifted apart: put the tree the
# corpus was actually built from FIRST, or the divergence check compares landed bodies against
# bytes that never landed. `_intake/<alias>/` is searched before anything in this file whenever the
# working copy has it, for exactly that reason.
#
# ENVIRONMENT ALTERNATIVE, for a machine that cannot keep a file (CI):
#   LUNAR_ORACLE_SOURCE_ROOTS="japanese-miracle=/srv/corpora/jm;another=/srv/corpora/x"
# Entries are <alias>=<path>, separated by the platform's path delimiter (`;` on Windows, `:`
# elsewhere). A bare path there is left to audit_abstract_overlap.js and is not given an alias.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------- CHECKING WHAT YOU JUST WROTE
#
#   node tools/verify_corpus.js --sources
#
# lists every `Source:` declaration in one of three states, and the three are never folded together:
#
#   RESOLVED            this machine can open the file.
#   UNRESOLVABLE-HERE   this machine has not been told where that tree is. NORMAL on a fresh clone.
#                       Never a failure.
#   BROKEN              the root IS configured and the file is not inside it. A real defect --
#                       either the declaration is wrong or the root points at the wrong tree.
#
# SRC-1 fails only on BROKEN. It reports VACUOUS, never OK, if nothing at all resolved: "no broken
# sources" over a population of zero resolvable ones is not a pass.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- TEMPLATE
# Delete these two examples and write your own. The paths below are placeholders and exist on
# nobody's machine; leaving them in place is harmless and reports `unresolvable here`.

# The Japanese-miracle economics corpus. Alias used by 25 summaries under literature/.
# as: japanese-miracle
/replace/with/the/absolute/path/to/your/japanese-miracle/source/tree

# An unaliased root: searched by filename by tools/audit_abstract_overlap.js, ignored by the
# alias resolver. Use this form for a pile of source PDFs that no summary names by alias.
/replace/with/the/absolute/path/to/another/read-only/source/tree
